	'cn_p1' => 'Allergy diagnosis is rarely about finding a single universal culprit; it is about examining how an individual\'s immune system reacts within their unique environment. Our published case notes illustrate how clinical histories uncover unexpected explanations for common symptoms.',
	'cn_p2' => 'Whether distinguishing birch pollen cross-reactivity from pet dander, identifying hidden food triggers linked with exercise, or isolating contact allergens in everyday watch straps, these summaries reflect our calm, evidence-based methodology. We publish these anonymised accounts to help patients understand what happens during a consultation, how tests are interpreted in context, and why a clear written avoidance plan transforms daily life without demanding extreme or unnecessary restrictions.',
	'cn_p3' => 'Among our recent records, you will find case studies covering diverse adult and paediatric presentations. One adult case explores how severe spring sneezing and eye irritation was traced to birch pollen rather than the family cat, allowing the pet to remain at home. Another outlines the investigation of exercise-induced hives triggered by specific meals before morning runs. In paediatric care, we document how a four-year-old safely navigated peanut reintroduction through supervised food challenges. We also share clinic operational notes, including how our Saturday morning testing clinics support busy working adults. All accounts reflect real clinical outcomes and verified medical observations from our Ghent rooms.',
	'co_band_h' => 'What we see',
	'co_band_l' => 'Seven families of trouble',
	'co_c1_p' => 'Seasonal allergic rhinitis, commonly known as hay fever, causes significant nasal congestion, sneezing, and itchy eyes triggered by tree, grass, or weed pollens. Year-round symptoms often stem from indoor triggers such as house dust mites or domestic animal dander.',
	'co_c1_t' => 'Hay fever',
	'co_c2_p' => 'Allergic asthma frequently coexists with rhinitis, where airway inflammation causes wheezing, chest tightness, and shortness of breath upon exposure to inhalant allergens or exercise. Our clinic combines skin-prick testing with lung function spirometry and FeNO measurements to map your specific respiratory triggers accurately.',
	'co_c2_t' => 'Asthma',
	'co_c3_p' => 'Atopic dermatitis, or eczema, causes dry, inflamed, itchy skin often aggravated by environmental triggers, sweating, irritants, or specific contact allergens. We employ diagnostic patch testing to differentiate between constitutional eczema and allergic contact dermatitis caused by metals, fragrances, or preservatives.',
	'co_c3_t' => 'Eczema',
	'co_c4_p' => 'Urticaria, or hives, presents as itchy, raised wheals across the body, appearing spontaneously or provoked by physical factors such as heat, exercise, cold, or medications.',
	'co_c4_t' => 'Hives',
	'co_c5_p' => 'Food allergies trigger immune responses ranging from oral itching and facial swelling to severe systemic anaphylaxis.',
	'co_c5_t' => 'Food allergy',
	'co_c6_p' => 'Drug allergies, including adverse reactions to antibiotics, local anaesthetics, or anti-inflammatory medications, are systematically investigated by Dr Amara Okoye to identify safe alternative pharmaceutical options.',
	'co_c6_t' => 'Drug allergy',
	'co_c7_p' => 'Insect venom allergy from bee or wasp stings carries significant risk of generalised reactions; we provide comprehensive venom evaluation, emergency adrenaline injector training, and subcutaneous venom immunotherapy protocols where indicated to protect patients working or spending time outdoors.',
